$OctSixteenArticles = @(
  @{
    Slug = 'void-vs-destructible-security-labels'
    Title = 'VOID vs Destructible Security Labels: Choose the Evidence You Need'
    Description = 'Compare VOID transfer and destructible security labels by opening method, surface, residue, artwork and real-package testing before ordering.'
    Category = 'Tamper-evident packaging'
    CardDescription = 'The useful question is what a buyer should see after someone tries to lift the seal.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of an original blue VOID security seal lifted from a plain electronics box beside a fragmented orange destructible seal, each with complete printed graphics'
    Caption = 'Compare the attempted opening on the actual package, not on a flat sample card.'
    Published = '2026-10-16'
    Faq = @(
      @('Are VOID and destructible labels interchangeable?', 'No. A VOID design is intended to reveal a transfer pattern when lifted under suitable conditions, while a destructible construction is intended to break apart. Results depend on the complete label and the application surface.'),
      @('Will either seal prove that nobody opened a package?', 'No. A seal is one part of a packaging and inspection process. Test the proposed construction on the actual carton, closure and handling route.'),
      @('Can a security label be applied across a fold?', 'It may be possible, but corners, coatings and opening forces can change the result. Test the final placement on packed samples before approval.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>The word "security" sounds reassuring on a quote sheet. It tells me very little about what the receiver will actually see. Imagine a small electronics carton: the closing flap lifts, and the warehouse needs to decide whether the carton has been opened. A label that looks impressive while intact but peels away without a useful trace has missed its job.</p>
  <p>I would start by asking what evidence the buyer expects after an attempted lift. A VOID-style construction aims to leave a visible transfer message or pattern. A destructible construction aims to fragment rather than come away in one neat piece. Neither description is a guarantee on every box. The face stock, adhesive, surface coating, time after application and opening motion work together.</p>
  <h2>Test the Seal on the Real Closure</h2>
  <p>Use the production carton or container, not only a supplier's demonstration sheet. Apply samples across the exact flap or cap position. Try an ordinary opening, a slow corner lift and handling after the pack has spent time in its expected storage environment. Photograph the intact seal and the attempted opening. Decide whether the residue or fragment is obvious to a person checking many packs quickly.</p>
  <p>A heavily coated carton, textured paper or dusty plastic may behave differently from smooth test film. If a seal bridges two moving panels, check whether normal shipping flex makes it fail before the customer opens the pack. That is a false alarm, and repeated false alarms make any evidence system less useful.</p>
  <h2>Design for Inspection, Not Decoration Alone</h2>
  <p>Print a complete original mark, a clear inspection cue and, where useful, an approved batch or serial field. Do not rely on tiny security wording that disappears at the final size. Keep the artwork away from the engineered evidence area unless the label supplier confirms the effect remains visible. A box may need a separate product label and a smaller security seal instead of forcing both jobs into one crowded sticker.</p>
  <p>The product owner should define the inspection rule: what counts as opened, who photographs the result and what happens to an exception. A tamper-evident label cannot establish chain of custody by itself, and it should not be marketed as tamper-proof.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send the actual packed carton and closure location</li><li>Describe the evidence the receiver must notice</li><li>Trial both constructions on the real surface</li><li>Check shipping flex and intended storage</li><li>Approve visible artwork at actual size</li><li>Document the inspection decision</li></ul>
  <p>Our <a href="/blog/tamper-evident-labels-guide/">tamper-evident label overview</a> covers the wider buying decision. For closure geometry, compare the <a href="/blog/jar-lid-tamper-seals-vs-shrink-bands/">jar seal and shrink-band guide</a>. Send the package, a photo of the closed flap and the desired evidence result through the <a href="/get-quote/">quote form</a>.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'applying-labels-to-frozen-packaging'
    Title = 'Applying Labels to Frozen Packaging: Label Before or After Freezing?'
    Description = 'Plan label application for frozen packs by comparing pre-freeze and cold-surface application, condensation, handling and trial conditions.'
    Category = 'Cold-chain labeling'
    CardDescription = 'A freezer-ready material does not answer when the packing line applies it.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of illustrated berry frozen-food pouches, one being labeled on a dry packing bench and one frosted in a freezer, with complete original label designs'
    Caption = 'Write the application step into the brief before choosing the label construction.'
    Published = '2026-10-16'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can any freezer label be applied to a frozen bag?', 'No. Storage performance and application performance are separate questions. Ask for a construction qualified for the actual surface and application temperature, then trial it.'),
      @('Should labels be applied before freezing?', 'Often that is easier to evaluate on a clean, dry pack, but it depends on the packing workflow and materials. Test the whole route rather than assuming one timing is always correct.'),
      @('Does condensation matter if the final pack stays frozen?', 'Yes. Moisture at the moment of application can interfere with contact. Record whether packs are dry, frosted or wet when labeling occurs.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>Two bags of frozen berries can reach the same freezer with very different label histories. One was labeled on a clean, dry pouch before filling and freezing. The other was pulled from cold storage and labeled while frost formed on the film. If a buyer asks only for a "freezer label," I still do not know which problem to solve.</p>
  <p>I would map the packing line first: when the bag is sealed, when it cools, when a date or lot code is printed, and when someone presses the label onto it. The application step matters because an adhesive has to make contact before anyone can judge how it behaves in storage. A claim about low-temperature storage is not automatically a claim about sticking to a cold or wet surface.</p>
  <h2>Compare the Two Application Routes</h2>
  <p>For pre-freeze application, trial the label on production film at the real packing-line speed. Check whether the bag wrinkles under the label and whether sealing or filling disturbs the edge. Then freeze, handle and thaw representative packs according to the actual distribution route. Do not evaluate the label only when the pouch is still flat and empty.</p>
  <p>For post-freeze application, record the pack surface temperature and visible moisture. A frosted bag, a dry frozen carton and a cold but damp tub are different test surfaces. Ask the supplier whether the proposed construction is suitable for that application condition. Press samples on finished packs and inspect them after the full planned hold and handling cycle. If frost makes the result inconsistent, changing the sequence may be more dependable than searching for a stronger-sounding adhesive.</p>
  <h2>Keep the Printed Information Useful</h2>
  <p>The front can carry a complete original food illustration and readable product name. Leave a clean field for the brand's approved ingredient, date, lot and barcode information. Test scanning after freezing and after condensation appears. If several flavors share one base artwork, proof each SKU and its data source separately. The food brand remains responsible for market-specific copy and claims.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Identify pouch, carton or tub material</li><li>State whether labeling is before or after freezing</li><li>Record surface temperature and moisture at application</li><li>Trial on filled finished packs</li><li>Check print, edges and barcode after handling</li><li>Approve each flavor and variable-data field</li></ul>
  <p>Read the broader <a href="/blog/freezer-labels-for-frozen-food-packaging/">freezer label buying guide</a> for material planning and the <a href="/blog/sticker-adhesive-surface-matching-guide/">surface-matching guide</a> for the adhesive conversation. Send photos of the actual packing stage with a <a href="/label-specification-planner/">label brief</a>. The timing is part of the specification.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'multilingual-shampoo-bottle-labels'
    Title = 'Multilingual Shampoo Bottle Labels: Make Room Without Shrinking the Type'
    Description = 'Plan multilingual shampoo labels around bottle curvature, approved market copy, front-panel hierarchy, small type and variant proofing.'
    Category = 'Cosmetic packaging'
    CardDescription = 'The second language is a layout decision, not a last-minute line added to the proof.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of three unbranded shampoo bottles with complete original botanical labels and legible bilingual front-panel words arranged beside printed proof sheets'
    Caption = 'Proof both languages on the actual bottle at normal reading distance.'
    Published = '2026-10-16'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can the printer translate ingredient and warning copy?', 'The responsible brand should provide and approve market-specific wording. A printer can identify layout pressure but should not invent translations or regulatory copy.'),
      @('Is a wrap label always the answer to extra languages?', 'No. A wrap can add area, but bottle curvature, overlap, application method and readability still need a physical proof.'),
      @('Can small type be checked on a screen proof?', 'Not reliably. Print at final size, apply to the final bottle and review it under ordinary lighting.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A shampoo label can look settled until a second market is added. The front panel still needs the product name; the back now has twice the words, and the bottle has not become any wider. The usual temptation is to shrink everything. I would stop there and ask which information a shopper must find first and which wording the brand has actually approved.</p>
  <p>Start with the physical bottle. A drawing of a flat label does not show where a curved shoulder hides the final line or where a pump blocks the top of the front panel. Measure the usable area after the bottle is filled and capped. If the design uses an original leaf or fruit illustration, keep it complete but give the product name enough space to do its job.</p>
  <h2>Separate Hierarchy From Translation</h2>
  <p>Make a content map for each destination market: approved product name, variant, ingredients, directions, caution text, responsible-party details and any other owner-supplied fields. The responsible brand or distributor must confirm what is required and approve every translation. The label supplier can flag a line that becomes too small or a field that falls into an overlap; it should not create legal copy to fill a template.</p>
  <p>Decide whether one bilingual artwork serves both markets or separate labels make more sense. One shared file simplifies inventory only when the full copy remains readable. Two versions add version-control work but may preserve clearer typography. Compare the cost and operational risk together instead of making the decision from print price alone.</p>
  <h2>Proof the Longest Variant on the Bottle</h2>
  <p>Use the longest product name and the longest approved translated line in a full-size print proof. Apply it to the actual bottle, not a generic cylinder. Check the label seam, the area under the pump, glare in wet light and the smallest type. Then proof each fragrance or formula separately so one variant does not accidentally inherit another variant's ingredient panel.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Supply final bottles and cap or pump</li><li>Provide approved text for each market</li><li>Compare shared versus separate artwork</li><li>Print the longest text at finished size</li><li>Inspect front, back and seam on the bottle</li><li>Track approvals by language and formula</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/custom-shampoo-bottle-labels-guide/">shampoo bottle guide</a> covers the base material and use conditions; the <a href="/blog/white-ink-small-text-custom-stickers/">small-type guide</a> helps with proof review. Send bottle dimensions and approved copy through the <a href="/get-quote/">quote form</a>. More text is manageable when it is treated as a packaging decision early.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'variable-data-batch-labels-small-runs'
    Title = 'Variable-Data Batch Labels for Small Runs: Keep Every SKU Traceable'
    Description = 'Plan short-run batch labels with stable artwork, variable lot and date fields, data ownership, scan checks and pack-out controls.'
    Category = 'Variable-data production'
    CardDescription = 'One wrong lot code can turn a tidy short run into a sorting job.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of original illustrated tea pouch labels with distinct flavor names and visible batch fields beside a small packing station and proof list'
    Caption = 'Match each printed code to its approved SKU row before packing.'
    Published = '2026-10-16'
    Faq = @(
      @('Should lot codes be printed with the artwork or added later?', 'Choose based on the brand''s data source, production sequence and inspection process. Both routes need a proof of the real finished pack.'),
      @('Can one generic label serve several flavors?', 'Only when the final applied label still identifies the correct product clearly and carries all brand-approved information. Control the variable fields and packing by SKU.'),
      @('Who decides the lot and date format?', 'The product owner should define the approved format and data. The printer should reproduce it accurately and help verify legibility, placement and count.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A short run can create more label versions than a large one. Three tea flavors, two market languages and a new lot each week sound manageable until a packing table holds six nearly identical rolls. I would make the variant list and the source of each code visible before discussing how attractive the label looks.</p>
  <p>Separate fixed artwork from data that changes. The original illustrated flavor design, product name and approved ingredient panel may be stable within a version. Lot, date or serial fields may change by batch or by unit. Write down who supplies each field, when the value is locked and how it reaches the print or coding step. A clean file name is helpful; a signed-off data table is better.</p>
  <h2>Choose the Coding Point</h2>
  <p>Some projects print variable data with the label. Others use a later thermal-transfer or inkjet step on a reserved field. The right route depends on the run size, packing workflow, label surface and the owner's equipment. Test the chosen code on the actual finished label and pack. A code can be readable on a flat proof yet land across a pouch fold or disappear against a dark illustration.</p>
  <p>If a barcode or QR code changes, test it with the intended scanner and data destination. Do not assume that a code that scans once under office lighting will scan on a curved or chilled pack. Keep a human-readable identifier beside machine-readable data when the operational process needs one.</p>
  <h2>Make Pack-Out Part of Quality Control</h2>
  <p>Keep one approved row per SKU, language and batch. Compare the first printed item with that row; repeat the check when a roll or data file changes. Mark finished bundles so packing staff can identify them without opening every sleeve. If a correction is made, retire the old file and unused labels deliberately. The brand is responsible for approved product and date information; the supplier's job is to follow and verify the agreed data flow.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>List every SKU, language and changing field</li><li>Define the owner and lock time for source data</li><li>Reserve a readable code area in artwork</li><li>Check first-off print and every changeover</li><li>Scan on finished packs where relevant</li><li>Label bundles and retire obsolete versions</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/custom-sticker-reorder-checklist/">reorder checklist</a> helps with version control, while the <a href="/blog/retail-barcode-labels-for-product-packaging/">barcode label guide</a> covers scanning. Send a sample data table, a finished pack and the coding workflow through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'refillable-glass-bottle-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Refillable Glass Bottle Labels: Design for Washing and Relabeling'
    Description = 'Plan labels for refillable glass bottles around wash cycles, residual adhesive, return sorting, brand artwork and relabeling tests.'
    Category = 'Reusable packaging'
    CardDescription = 'The first application is easy to admire; the next wash tells you whether the system works.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of reusable amber glass bottles with complete original citrus and herb labels beside a wash basin and a clean relabeled bottle'
    Caption = 'Test removal and the next application on bottles that have completed the real wash.'
    Published = '2026-10-16'
    Faq = @(
      @('Does removable adhesive guarantee residue-free washing?', 'No. Glass finish, wash chemistry, dwell time, label construction and handling all affect removal. Test the real bottle and process.'),
      @('Can a permanent label stay through repeated washes?', 'That depends on the construction and washing conditions. Define whether the artwork should remain or be replaced before choosing materials.'),
      @('Who verifies food-contact or reuse claims?', 'The product owner must assess the bottle, washing process and applicable market requirements. A decorative label alone does not establish compliance.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A refillable bottle label has two lives. The first is on a full shelf, where the illustrated citrus or herb design helps someone pick the right product. The second is at the wash station, where someone has to decide whether that label stays, comes off cleanly or leaves a sticky ring for the next cycle. I would ask about the second life before approving the first artwork.</p>
  <p>Define the reuse model. Is the bottle refilled at home, returned to a shop or washed in a central facility? Does the brand keep a permanent front mark and replace only a small batch label, or remove the whole wrap each time? These choices change adhesive, face material, ink protection and how much work the relabeling step creates.</p>
  <h2>Test the Removal Route</h2>
  <p>Use the final glass bottle with its real surface finish. Apply labels using the intended pressure and allow the usual dwell before washing. Then follow the operator's actual wash method. Observe edge lift, tearing, residue and how long removal takes. A label that peels beautifully after one hour may behave differently after weeks on a filled bottle. Do not promise residue-free removal without a representative trial.</p>
  <p>If the front label is meant to stay, check repeated wet handling and washing instead. Inspect print rub, adhesive edges and whether a new variable-data label can be applied without creating a layered mess. Do not use a broad "washable" claim until the project owner and supplier have agreed what wash cycle is being tested.</p>
  <h2>Make the Next Fill Easy to Identify</h2>
  <p>Keep the complete original product illustration and a strong product name, but reserve a practical field for the current variant, fill date or other owner-approved information. Return sorting matters: a similar-looking herb and citrus bottle should not rely on a tiny color shift alone. Test relabeling on bottles after they have been washed and dried, then check whether the fresh label sits flat on the cleaned surface.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Define keep-or-remove for each label panel</li><li>Provide final glass bottles and wash method</li><li>Trial realistic dwell before removal</li><li>Inspect residue, print and operator time</li><li>Proof current variant and data fields</li><li>Apply a new label after the real wash</li></ul>
  <p>See the <a href="/blog/removable-vs-permanent-stickers/">removable versus permanent guide</a> and the <a href="/blog/custom-refill-jar-labels-guide/">refill jar label guide</a> for related material choices. Share bottle photos, washing details and the intended number of reuse cycles in the <a href="/get-quote/">project brief</a>. The best label is the one the next operator can work with.</p>
'@
  }
)
